<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Haitian Revolution</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1791">1791</a>–<a href="/years/1804">1804</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Haitian Revolution of 1791–1804 was an uprising of enslaved people in the French colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a> that led to the abolition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> there and the creation of the independent state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a>. It was the only successful large-scale slave revolt to found a state. For the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">French Empire</a> it meant the loss of its richest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> colony.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/haitian-revolution.jpg" alt="Haitian Revolution" width="320" height="217" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Aaron Martinet / Alphonse-Charles Masson · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Incendie_de_la_Plaine_du_Cap._Massacre_des_Blancs_par_les_esclaves_noirs_r%C3%A9volt%C3%A9s._France_militaire._Martinet_et_Masson.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In 1789 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a> produced 60 percent of the world's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> and 40 percent of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> imported by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, and its largest sugar plantations and concentrations of enslaved people lay in the north. Enslaved people were kept in check by whipping and worse punishments. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/code-noir">Code Noir</a> of 1685, issued to regulate the treatment of the enslaved, was openly and consistently broken. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/declaration-of-the-rights-of-man-and-of-the-citizen">Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen</a> of 26 August 1789 was ambiguous about slaves and colonial citizens. After the revolt of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vincent-oge">Vincent Ogé</a> in 1790, France granted citizenship to free people of colour in May 1791. White planters refused to comply, fighting between the groups broke out, and on 21 August 1791 the enslaved rose.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> declared war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in 1793, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grands-blancs">grands blancs</a> asked British help, and Britain invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a>, as did <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. The French commissioners <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leger-felicite-sonthonax">Sonthonax</a> and Étienne Polverel offered freedom to enslaved people who would fight for them and in 1793 proclaimed abolition in the colony's provinces. On 6 May 1794 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toussaint-louverture">Toussaint Louverture</a> turned from the Spanish to the French. The British sent about 30,000 men in 1795 but suffered heavily from disease, and at least three of every five British troops sent between 1791 and 1797 died of it. The British left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/port-au-prince">Port-au-Prince</a> on 18 May 1798, and Toussaint defeated the forces of Rigaud in the War of the South, taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacmel">Jacmel</a> on 11 March 1800.</p><p>A French expedition arrived at Le Cap on 2 February 1802. Dessalines held the fort of Crête-à-Pierrot for about twenty days before slipping out on 24 March. Toussaint agreed to terms on 6 May 1802, was later seized and shipped to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and died in prison at Fort-de-Joux. French plans to restore <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> brought a new revolt in the summer of 1802, while yellow fever killed about 10,000 French by mid-July and the commander Leclerc in November. After war between France and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> resumed on 18 May 1803, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> blockaded the colony. On 30 November 1803, 8,000 French soldiers boarded British ships under terms agreed with Dessalines.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>On 1 January 1804 Dessalines declared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a> a free republic. From February until 22 April 1804, on his order, the remaining white colonists were massacred. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> recognised independence in 1825 only on condition that Haiti pay an indemnity of 150 million francs to former slaveholders. Estimates of the dead vary: Robert Scheina gave 350,000 Haitians and 50,000 European troops, and the Encyclopedia of African American Politics nearly 200,000 Black people and as many as 100,000 French and British soldiers, with yellow fever the largest cause. Historians such as David Geggus judge the effect on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> limited, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-e-b-du-bois">W. E. B. Du Bois</a> held that it made British abolition easier.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Haitian Revolution” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Haitian Revolution</b> of 1791 to 1804 destroyed Saint-Domingue and produced the first Black republic in history under the leadership of Toussaint Louverture and Jean-Jacques Dessalines.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-empire">French Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Its support for Simón Bolívar helped the liberation of Venezuela and northern South America, and Pétion's request that Bolívar abolish slavery linked the <b>Haitian Revolution</b> to emancipation on the mainland, although the new Spanish American republics did not invite Haiti to the Congress of Panama in 1826.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-haiti-1806-1849">Republic of Haiti (1806-1849) · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Many Vodouists took part in the <b>Haitian Revolution</b> of 1791 to 1801.</blockquote><a href="/religion/haitian-vodou">Haitian Vodou · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Quaker and evangelical campaigners, Black writers and the enslaved themselves, through petitions, boycotts, escapes and revolts such as the <b>Haitian Revolution</b>, forced governments to act, though freedom rarely brought the formerly enslaved land, compensation or equal rights.</blockquote><a href="/movement/abolitionism">Abolitionism · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>It did not mention slavery, yet it inspired the uprisings in Saint-Domingue in the <b>Haitian Revolution</b>, as C. L. R. James discusses in The Black Jacobins, and the organisers of the Pointe Coupée Slave Conspiracy of 1795 in Louisiana also drew on it.</blockquote><a href="/work/declaration-of-the-rights-of-man-and-of-the-citizen">Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1903, to mark the 100th anniversary of the <b>Haitian Revolution</b>, a competition was held for a national anthem.</blockquote><a href="/songs/la-dessalinienne">La Dessalinienne</a></li></ul></section>
